  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Hyakunin Isshu. Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Hyakunin Isshu (ç™¾äººä¸€é¦– - 'One Hundred People, One Poem Each') is a traditional Japanese card game based on a classical anthology of waka poems. It is played as a competitive game called 'Karuta'. Players must match a poem being read aloud with the correct 'grabbing' card spread out on the floor. It is a game of extreme speed, memory, and auditory processing.",
    objective: 'Empty your territory of cards before your opponent.',
    gameplay: "1. A reader ('Yomite') reads the first half (upper phrase) of a poem.\n2. Both players attempt to touch the grabbing card containing the second half (lower phrase).\n3. If you grab a card in your own territory, it is removed.\n4. If you grab a card in your opponent's territory, you send one card from your territory to theirs.\n5. **Otetsuki (Fault)**: Touching the wrong card results in a penalty (opponent sends a card).",
    keyRules: [
      'Reader recites the first phrase of a poem; first to touch the matching Torifuda wins that card. Correct touch in your territory removes the card; correct touch in opponent territory lets you send one card from your territory to theirs. Wrong touch (otetsuki) gives the opponent a card. First to empty their territory wins.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Typically 1 vs 1 in competitive play.',
      deck: '100 poetry cards. In a match, only 50 are used (25 for each player).',
      equipment: 'Uta-garuta deck (200 cards: 100 Yomifuda reading cards, 100 Torifuda grabbing cards), playing mat or flat surface, reader (yomite) to read the poems.',
      dealing: 'Shuffle the 100 Torifuda (grabbing cards). Each player places 25 cards in their territory (face-up). The remaining 50 Torifuda are not used in that round. The Yomifuda (reading deck) is kept separate for the reader.',
    },
    turnFlow: "1. A reader ('Yomite') reads the first half (upper phrase) of a poem.\n2. Both players attempt to touch the grabbing card containing the second half (lower phrase).\n3. If you grab a card in your own territory, it is removed.\n4. If you grab a card in your opponent's territory, you send one card from your territory to theirs.\n5. **Otetsuki (Fault)**: Touching the wrong card results in a penalty (opponent sends a card).",
